---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { evidenceUrl, url } from "../lib/specs";
const rows = [
 ["Frequency coverage", "Stock V4: approximately 500 kHz–1.766 GHz, including its HF upconverter.", "Best starting point: normal 2.4 GHz band. Upstream reports experiments around 2.2–2.7 GHz; extended tuning commands do not prove RF coverage."],
 ["Sampling", "Continuous 8-bit I + 8-bit Q over USB. Start near 2.4 MS/s; measure loss before increasing it.", "Nominal 16/40/80 MS/s snapshots, 8/10-bit output; at most 16,380 complex pairs on the original ESP32."],
 ["Time captured", "Can record seconds or hours when the host and storage keep up.", "At maximum snapshot length: about 1.024 / 0.410 / 0.205 ms at 16 / 40 / 80 MS/s, followed by processing and transport gaps."],
 ["Instantaneous spectrum", "A few MHz. Good for narrowband channels and continuous demodulation.", "Tens of MHz per snapshot, depending on sampling and filters. Useful for observing wide 2.4 GHz channels; not a guarantee of calibrated bandwidth."],
 ["RF front end", "SMA connector, manufacturer-documented filtering and 1 ppm TCXO. Gain and antenna choices are accessible.", "Wi-Fi RF path and board antenna. Gain, filter mapping, frequency accuracy and actual antenna behavior need characterization."],
 ["Applications", "HF listening, broadcast FM, airband, AIS, ADS-B, 433 MHz sensors, spectrum monitoring; software and antennas depend on the signal.", "Wi-Fi-channel occupancy experiments, interference snapshots, controlled short bursts, teaching FFTs and receiver design. Protocol decoding needs separate evidence."],
 ["Transmission", "Receive-only in the normal SDR configuration.", "ESP-SDR currently implements reception. The chip's normal Wi-Fi transmitter is a separate capability, not an arbitrary I/Q transmitter."],
 ["Confirmed here", "USB probe identifies Blog V4 + R828D. Sustained reception and sample loss remain unmeasured.", "ROM query identifies ESP32-D0WD-V3 rev 3.1 with 4 MB flash. ESP-SDR and RF reception have not yet been tested."]
];
---
<Base title="Two receivers, different jobs"><header class="page-title"><p class="eyebrow">RTL-SDR BLOG V4 / ORIGINAL ESP32</p><h1>Keep both receivers.<br />They cover different jobs.</h1><p class="lede">Your V4 is the practical continuous receiver for HF, VHF and UHF. The ESP32 offers experimental access to wide slices around 2.4 GHz, where the V4 cannot tune.</p><p><a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/rtl-identification/README.md")}>V4 identity evidence</a> · <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/board-identification/README.md")}>ESP32 identity evidence</a></p></header>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="lab-table"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Attached RTL-SDR Blog V4</th><th>Attached ESP32 + proposed ESP-SDR</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(row => <tr>{row.map((cell,i) => i === 0 ? <th scope="row">{cell}</th> : <td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<section class="section split"><article class="note-box"><h3>Compare the same signal carefully</h3><p>Their useful stock bands do not overlap. A same-signal comparison needs a characterized frequency converter or separate calibrated sources. A 10-bit ESP output does not establish better dynamic range than an 8-bit RTL receiver.</p><a href={url("work/?work=the-two-receivers-have-a-measured-division-of-labor")}>Open the comparison experiment →</a></article><article class="note-box"><h3>What is established today?</h3><p>The V4 probe set 2.048 MS/s during identification, but that is not a sustained streaming test. Likewise, an upstream ESP-SDR success does not prove our board receives correctly. The evaluation records drop counts, capture gaps, signal conditions and repeatability.</p><a href={url("ledger/")}>See the accepted observations →</a></article></section>
<section class="section prose-note"><p>Primary references: <a href="https://www.rtl-sdr.com/rtl-sdr-blog-v4-dongle-initial-release/">V4 manufacturer specifications</a>, <a href="https://www.rtl-sdr.com/about-rtl-sdr/">RTL sampling and applications</a>, <a href="https://github.com/ESPARGOS/esp-sdr/tree/550fadea4d00a9e26ce921c5832167becb3dc20c">pinned ESP-SDR firmware</a> and <a href="https://github.com/ESPARGOS/esp-sdr/blob/550fadea4d00a9e26ce921c5832167becb3dc20c/docs/rx-controls.md">RF-control limitations</a>.</p></section></Base>
